# Mid-turn Ctrl+C cancel and recovery. Send a long-running prompt, fire
# <C-c> while the turn is in flight, then issue a fresh prompt. The second
# turn proves the agent loop recovered: a regression in the cancel +
# reminder-queue path would either leave the second prompt unsent or trip
# up the streaming state machine.
#
# Ctrl+C is the real cancel hotkey in zag (see EventOrchestrator.zig).
# Esc only enters normal mode in the modal keymap; it does NOT abort a
# running agent.
#
# Robustness notes for reasoning models:
#   * "turn in flight" is detected via the "* Working..." line zag renders
#     while the agent runs. wait_idle can't see it (a reasoning model
#     streams continuously, so there is never a quiet window).
#   * Recovery is proven by turn LIFECYCLE (Working appears, then the grid
#     settles), not by a token in the prompt: any token the prompt names
#     also renders in the submitted user-message bubble, so wait_text on it
#     would false-match the echo rather than the tool output.
#   * The cancel is allowed to fully settle before the recovery prompt, so
#     the recovery <Enter> isn't sent into the cancel teardown.

spawn ./zig-out/bin/zag
wait_text /Welcome to zag/
wait_idle 500ms
send "Write a 500-word essay about the history of pseudo-terminals on UNIX. Be thorough." <Enter>
wait_text /Working/ 30s
send <C-c>
# Let the cancel fully settle (working line gone, grid quiet) before the
# recovery prompt, so the <Enter> lands as a fresh submit rather than racing
# the in-progress cancel teardown.
wait_idle 2s 20s
send "Forget that. Just run bash 'echo zag-recover-token-T7'." <Enter>
# Recovery turn must START (proves the prompt submitted and the agent loop
# is alive after the cancel) and then FINISH (a full post-cancel turn).
wait_text /Working/ 30s
wait_idle 2s 60s
snapshot final
send "/quit" <Enter>
# Teardown after a streaming session is slower than the 10s default.
wait_exit 30s
